/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (Regulation is the priority: 89% of all responses on how democracy could survive AI focused on five key areas: regulation \(30%\), education \(16%\), collective action \(14%\), accountability \(14%\), and individual action \(9%\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (Strong appetite for accountability: Among participants, there was a clear appetite for responsibility to sit with those who design, deploy, and profit from AI systems. 82% of accountability-focused responses centered on holding tech executives or companies responsible.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (Workplaces as sites of resistance: Participants viewed AI as having the most potential, but also the greatest risk, in the workplace. 50% of collective action responses focused on worker-based action, particularly through unions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (Collective action and resistance: 14% of responses referenced collective action, with notable themes around community-owned AI as a form of collective resistance. An additional 9% focused on individual resistance actions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
