184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (Of the 14% of responses that mentioned accountability as a way to protect democracy from AI-related harms, over half \(53%\) focused on holding tech executives accountable. These responses emphasised the consequences that CEOs and senior leaders should face when their products undermine democratic processes. A further 26% argued that holding big tech companies accountable for the risks their systems pose would help safeguard democracy.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (The strong focus on individual and corporate responsibility is an important insight into how our tech-savvy participants are conceptualising AI harms. Taken together, 82% of all accountability-themed responses centred on companies or their staff. This suggests a clear appetite for responsibility to sit with the actors who design, deploy or profit from AI systems - a sentiment that could be leveraged to shape future public messaging and policy advocacy.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (By contrast, accountability for AI systems, societal actors and for businesses misusing AI each only received 3% of responses. Responses tagged as referencing general accountability tended to mention the concept in passing, without specifying who should be held responsible.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
