<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (Individual actions refer to choices one person could undertake to mitigate the effect of AI on democracy. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
192 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (32% of all responses centred on vigilance with content consumption. Suspecting all media is AI until proven otherwise shifts the burden onto individuals rather than companies. However, in the face of tech inaction, it can be a way for users to regain control over the media they consume. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
193 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (28% of responses regarding individual actions involved the idea that re-framing AI would help protect democracy from its harms. Some believed that remaining empathetic was key, whereas others felt that readjusting expectations surrounding the availability of information would protect democracy. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
194 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (The idea that users could protect democracy through their use of AI was also significant, accounting for 24% of individual action-based responses. People believed that AI could be used responsibly for socially beneficial ends.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
195 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (Some participants suggested that if individuals changed the way they engage with politics, it could help protect democracy from AI-driven harms \(4%\). Others emphasised the role of strong community leadership \(4%\). Notably, 8% expressed the view that human confidence itself could safeguard democracy. This belief frames AI risks as, in part, a problem of diminished self-efficacy.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
